Agent Performance, Case & Ticket Management System – SQL Data Exploration and Analysis

Project Overview
This project explores and analyzes a case and ticket management system to understand how tickets are created, 
updated, categorized, and resolved by exploring the tickets and analyzing patterns and how agent performance impacts resolution efficiency and customer satisfaction.
Classify agents according to their performance and evaluate their contributions.

The analysis focuses on operational insights derived from structured data using SQL, covering:
- Statistical analysis to use as benchmark in ticket logs
- Time period for an open ticket to open and close. min, avg, max days
- Update activity and trends
- Ticket categorization through tags
- Agent workload, efficiency, and CSAT

This project demonstrates my ability to explore data, ask the right questions, and translate SQL outputs into meaningful insights, 
even without prior industry experience.

Tools & Technologies
- SQL (SQL Server–style syntax)
- Aggregation functions (COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX)
- Group By, Order By
- Conditional logic (CASE)
- Subqueries & cross joins
- Temporary tables for reusable analysis

Dataset Description

The analysis uses four related tables
1. Case_Ticket_Management_TicketLogCSV
 Column;
  TicketID, CustomerID, AgentID, ProductId, Channel, Priority, Status, DateCreated, DateClosed, 
  ResolutionTimeHours, FirstResponseTimeHours
2. Case_Ticket_Management_TicketTagsCSV
 Columns;
  TaggingID, TicketID, Tag
3. Case_Ticket_Management_Details_UpdateCSV
 Columns;
  DetailID, TicketID, UpdateTimeStamp, UpdateText
4. Agent_Team_PerformanceCSV
 Columns;
  AgentID, AgentName, TicketClosed, Average_Resolution_Time_Hours, Average_CSAT_Score

The data was in a clean state; no additional string cleaning or trimming was required.


